[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
GPU rendering in Cycles

Without an NVIDIA GPU (AMD or Intel graphics) there is nothing to set up:
blender starts normally, the viewport and EEVEE use the GPU through Mesa and
Cycles renders on the CPU.  AMD HIP (ROCm) and Intel oneAPI (SYCL) are not
available on FreeBSD.  The rest of this message is for NVIDIA GPUs only.

NVIDIA CUDA rendering in Cycles (CYCLES_CUDA option)

FreeBSD's nvidia-driver has no native CUDA library, so the blender wrapper
loads the Linux one through nv-sglrun from emulators/libc6-shim.  Install
libc6-shim and the Linux NVIDIA libraries of exactly the same version as the
loaded kernel module (see `sysctl hw.nvidia.version`):

  All GPUs:
    emulators/libc6-shim
  GeForce GTX 9xx/10xx (Maxwell, Pascal) and Volta:
    x11/nvidia-driver-580 + x11/linux-nvidia-libs-580
  Newer GPUs:
    x11/nvidia-driver + x11/linux-nvidia-libs

The linux-nvidia-libs packages conflict with each other, remove the one that
does not match first.  libc6-shim also needs the Linux compatibility layer:

  sysrc linux_enable=YES && service linux start

Then select the GPU in Edit > Preferences > System > Cycles Render Devices
> CUDA.  OptiX is not available on FreeBSD.  When an AMD or Intel GPU drives
the display (amdgpu, radeonkms or i915kms loaded), OpenGL and Vulkan stay on
it and the NVIDIA GPU is used for CUDA only.

Set BLENDER_NO_NV_SGLRUN=1 to start blender without nv-sglrun.  If no GPU is
listed, start it from a terminal to see why:

  BLENDER_NV_DEBUG=1 blender --debug-cycles --log-level info
EOM
}
]
